This handler was written to fix a lost-update report. Under load, the report came back.

```js
app.patch('/jobs/:id', async (req, reply) => {
  const { expectedRevision, changes } = req.body;

  return db.$transaction(async (tx) => {
    const job = await tx.job.findUnique({ where: { id: req.params.id } });
    if (!job) return reply.status(404).send();

    if (job.revision !== expectedRevision) {
      return reply.status(409).send({ server: job });
    }

    const saved = await tx.job.update({
      where: { id: req.params.id },
      data: { ...changes, revision: job.revision + 1 },
    });
    return reply.send(saved);
  });
});
```

The revision is compared, the work is in a transaction, and a 409 is returned. Which statement identifies the remaining defect?

solution.explanationMarkdown:
Two concurrent requests can both read revision 7, both pass the `if`, and both call `update`. The second one overwrites the first, and both clients receive 200. The revision check is real but it is not *atomic with the write*.

The transaction does not close this. Under PostgreSQL's default Read Committed isolation the `findUnique` in each transaction sees the state at the time of that statement, and neither takes a lock that would make the other wait. Wrapping read-check-write in `BEGIN`/`COMMIT` groups the statements; it does not make the value read in the first statement stay true through the third.

The fix is to move the assertion into the write, so the database evaluates it and performs the update as one operation:

```js
const result = await tx.job.updateMany({
  where: { id: req.params.id, revision: expectedRevision },
  data: { crewId: changes.crewId, revision: { increment: 1 } },
});
if (result.count === 0) { /* 404 or 409 - re-read to tell which */ }
```

Note the second change: spreading `...changes` into `data` lets a client set any column, including `revision` itself. Naming the permitted fields is not a style preference here — it is what stops a client from choosing its own token.
